# Commit messages

Spec: https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/

Every commit message uses this structure:

```
<type>[optional scope][optional !]: <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

## Types

Lowercase. Only these:

- `feat` — a new feature (SemVer MINOR)
- `fix` — a bug fix (SemVer PATCH)
- `docs` — documentation only
- `style` — formatting; behavior stays the same
- `refactor` — structural change that preserves behavior
- `perf` — performance
- `test` — tests only
- `build` — build system or dependencies
- `ci` — continuous integration
- `chore` — other work that does not change product behavior
- `revert` — reverts an earlier commit; a `Refs:` footer lists the reverted SHAs

`feat` is a new feature. `fix` is a bug fix. Work that fits more than one type is split into separate commits.

## Subject, body, footer

- A scope, when present, is a lowercase noun in parentheses: `feat(user):`
- `!` immediately before `:` marks a breaking change
- The description is English, imperative, has no trailing period, and stays near 72 characters
- The body starts one blank line after the description and explains why
- A breaking change uses `!` and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer (uppercase token)
- Other footers use `Token: value` or `Token #value`; spaces in the token become `-`

## Examples

```
feat(user): deactivate a user and delete that user's sessions

fix(session): reject a session after its seven-day expiry

feat(auth)!: bind session lifetime to the server clock

BREAKING CHANGE: the client-side session lifetime is now ignored
```
